  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J10", "U2"],
      "pins": ["J10.A6", "J10.A7", "J10.B6", "J10.B7", "U2.27", "U2.26"],
      "nets": ["NET0009", "NET0010"],
      "obs": "On the mcu.kicad_sch sheet (top right), the USB-C receptacle J10 data pins are wired so that NET0009 carries both a D+ pin (A6) and a D- pin (B7), and NET0010 carries both a D- pin (A7) and a D+ pin (B6). The two label boxes sit at the two D- rows (A7, B7) and the D+ rows (A6, B6) are jumpered into those same two nets, producing a visible wire crossing. NET0009 also drives U2 pin 27 (GPIO19/USB_D+) and NET0010 drives U2 pin 26 (GPIO18/USB_D-).",
      "expected": "Standard USB-C wiring (and the spec's USB-C programming interface) requires the two D+ pins (A6, B6) to share one net and the two D- pins (A7, B7) to share a separate net, so the ESP32-C3 USB_D+ and USB_D- lines remain a proper differential pair. Instead D+ and D- are shorted together on each net.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch top-right: J10 A7->NET0010, B7->NET0009, A6->NET0009, B6->NET0010; U2 pin27 GPIO19/USB_D+->NET0009, pin26 GPIO18/USB_D-->NET0010"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Accepts power from either a 12 V barrel jack ... or USB-C 5 V (J12, logic/programming supply) ... USB-C CC1/CC2 pins go through R1/R7 (5.1 kΩ each) to GND"}
      ],
      "impact": "The USB-C differential pair is shorted (D+ tied to D-), so the ESP32-C3 will fail to enumerate over USB; programming and USB serial communication are broken.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build's topology otherwise matches the specification (power mux, buck, smart high-side heater switch, three shared-SPI MAX31855 channels, CC pulldowns), but on the MCU sheet the USB-C D+/D- data pins are cross-connected so that each net carries one D+ and one D-, shorting the differential pair and breaking USB."
}

